🎛️
"Instruments"/en
=== start/en
¶The same eight bars, played by every instrument in the palette. Choose a name
and the melody keeps its place — only the sound changes. The two cats are the
same cat, and so are the two dogs: one of each is a kit, so the tune reads as
noises, and the other is pitched, so it sings it.

Most of these are real recordings. The four synthesizers are not imitating
anything: they are synthesizers, and that is the sound. The last of them sings,
which is what the words on the track are for — every other instrument here
ignores them.¶

¶Which instrument the melody is playing. Choice() gives back the name of
whatever selectable output was picked, and ø until something is.¶
picked: Choice()

¶One entry per instrument: what to call it, what plays it, and the colour its
button takes.¶
•Voice(label•'' icon•'' sound•🔈 hue•#°)

voices•[Voice]: [
	Voice('piano' '🎹' 🔈.🎹 260°)
	Voice('guitar' '🎸' 🔈.🎸 20°)
	Voice('electric' '⚡' 🔈.⚡ 5°)
	Voice('violin' '🎻' 🔈.🎻 40°)
	Voice('flute' '🪈' 🔈.🪈 180°)
	Voice('trumpet' '🎺' 🔈.🎺 50°)
	Voice('sax' '🎷' 🔈.🎷 70°)
	Voice('harmonica' '🌬️' 🔈.🌬️ 150°)
	Voice('bell' '🔔' 🔈.🔔 55°)
	Voice('drums' '🥁' 🔈.🥁 0°)
	Voice('didgeridoo' '🎶' 🔈.didgeridoo 15°)
	Voice('cat' '🐱' 🔈.🐱 330°)
	Voice('tuned cat' '😺' 🔈.😺 350°)
	Voice('dog' '🐶' 🔈.🐶 35°)
	Voice('tuned dog' '🐕' 🔈.🐕 125°)
	Voice('synth' '🎛️' 🔈.🎛️ 290°)
	Voice('bass' '🎛️' 🔈.synthBass 310°)
	Voice('pad' '🎛️' 🔈.synthPad 200°)
	Voice('voice' '🤖' 🔈.🤖 95°)
]

¶The chosen voice, or the piano before anyone has chosen. find() gives back ø
when nothing matches, so the fallback has to be a Voice of its own.¶
chosen: voices.find(ƒ(voice•Voice) voice.label = picked)
current•Voice: chosen•ø ? Voice('piano' '🎹' 🔈.🎹 260°) chosen

¶A melody that stays put while the instrument underneath it changes. Degrees,
not notes, so it means the same thing on every instrument — and on the drums
it reads as rhythm instead, since degrees index the kit there.¶
tune: [1 3 5 6 5 3 2 ø]

¶One syllable per note, spelled in the International Phonetic Alphabet, where
each letter is one sound. Only the singing voice uses these; the rest of the
palette ignores them. The rest takes no syllable, so seven sounds cover eight
entries.¶
lyric: 'la li lu le lo li la'

¶One button per instrument: the icon stacked above its name, so they don't
run into each other. Selectable phrases are what Choice() reads, and the
current one is drawn brighter so you can see where you are.¶
ƒ button(voice•Voice)
	Group(
		Stack(padding: 0.1m)
		[
			Phrase(voice.icon 1.1m)
			Phrase(voice.label 0.5m)
		]
		name: voice.label
		selectable: ⊤
		color: (voice.label = current.label)
			? Color(95% 60 voice.hue)
			Color(60% 8 voice.hue)
	)

¶Everything visible goes in one Stack. A Stage centres each of its children
on its own, so left loose they would all sit on top of each other — the
layout has to say they go one above the next.

The buttons go in a Grid rather than a Row. All of them in one row is wider
than a phone, and a Stage scales its contents down to fit, so the labels
shrink until they can't be read. Only the columns are given: leave the rows
out and the Grid works out how many it needs, so adding an instrument
doesn't mean editing this.

The music keeps one name the whole time rather than taking the instrument's.
A 🎼's name is its identity: rename it and the player hears a different piece
begin, so the melody would jump back to the top on every switch, and the score
would draw the old instrument's notes beside the new one until they scrolled
away.¶
🎭(
	[
		🔳(
			Stack(padding: 0.6m)
			[
				💬(current.icon 2.5m)
				💬(current.label 1.2m)
				🔳(
					Grid(columns: 5 padding: 0.4m)
					voices.translate(ƒ(voice•Voice) button(voice))
				)
			]
		)
		🎼(
			🎶(tune instrument: current.sound words: lyric)
			tempo: 120beats/min
			scale: 🎼.pentatonic
			name: 'tune'
		)
	]
	background: Color(14% 12 280°)
	color: Color(95% 0 0°)
)
